IBM history: From cheese tailoring to slot flights
Found a hundred years ago in New York, IBM (International Business Machines) initially had a rather complicated name - Computing Tabulating recording corporation.
It didn't acquire the name IBM until in 1924 - it was originally concerned with a trademark name for a branch office in Canada and South America.
Found the foundation steps for what may later became an IBM company merged with four companies whose history dates back until the '80s in the 19th centuries.
They possessed technologies such as the famous stage card clock to include how to start and deserve.
Charles Ranlett Flint became an architecture of this new company, who also served as chairman of the Supervisory Board until 1930.
It was Flint who hired the head of competing company Thomas J. Watson to help him with head of the company.
This leader even eclipsed the founders of IBM - Watson's ideas continue to enjoy the invincible culture and identity of the company to this day.
Watson headed the company until 1952.
Like the name suggests, IBM started producing and distributing any machine for retail – from sausage and cheese tailoring, including coffers.
During the early four decades in which Watson headed the company, he doubled its sales to nine million dollars, gradually expanding on all continents.
Watson was so indispensable for the company because he spent forty years in his executive chair, only then he was succeeded by his son.
Under this leadership, IBM started working to develop artificial intelligence and also developed the first programming vocabulary.
During the 60's technicians and machines from IBM helped to switch NASA flights into space - to Mercury and Saturn, and in 1969 the famous flight of man to the moon.
Meanwhile, in 1964 IBM developed the first computer family with the same computing architecture - the IBM System/360 range.
A classic pPC that determined the computer standard of the time came on the rise in 1981 under the name IBM 5150.
Twenty many years later IBM sold its calculated production to the Chinese company Lenovo, where on the other hand it purchased the consulting body of PricewaterhouseCoopers and thus fully penetrated business with consulting services with high added value.
