Travel price development since 1989
Until 1989 there were only five approved state travel agencies at that time at that time: edok, sports manager, CKM, Rekrea and autoturist.
The travel agencies merely swept travel to the socialist Eastern Bloc countries at that time, and at that time they were entitled chosen.
Czech tourists travelled to the Baltic Sea (to the German Democratic Republic - the GDR), the Sochi on the peninsula of the Crimea on the Black Sea), to Bulgaria and Romania to the Black Sea and to Hungary at Lake Balaton.
An exempt exception occurred from 1962 until 1972, when travel to Yugoslavia, when Yugoslavia became a pro-Western course and therefore trips to Yugoslavia severely became severely restricted.
A great paradox of those socialist days was that not even citizens of the eastern bloc (including Czechoslovakians) were allowed to travel freely within the socialist countries.
For example, if a citizen wanted to travel to Poland or the Soviet Soviet Union individual individually, he needed a so-called invitation letter, otherwise individual journeys would not be possible.
Already whereas the travel agenci agencies offered trips to the vest, but then one had to accept huge queues or even stay over slaughter in front of the travel travel travel agency during sleeping bags, respectively at least "good acquaintances".
This included the matter by no means went.
The so-called currencies promise - the condition for leaving the way to the West - were granted only "politically reliable" people - the others were not allowed to travel at all.
The Czechoslovakian ceremony was not a freely exchangeable currency, so it would have been impossible to draw up the necessary financial resources (Western currency) to travel to the West.
This is why most Czechs spent holidays domestically, no wonder the Czechs bought countless weekend houses and night stores - a habit which continues until this day.
The velvet revolution of 1989 followed a travel-revolution of the Czechs straight away.
Whereas until 1989 a holiday happened in the GDR (Govern or in Bulgaria), last year Czechs spent 4.5 million their holidays abroad in countries all around the world.
A tremendous difference, which one no longer conscereed today, is the fact that one could not travel at this day at the most most popular holiday destination of the Czechs - Croatia until 1989, because it was a part of yesteryugoslavia at that time.
Immediate from the beginning of the twenty-few years from the beginning of the last century, foreign currencies were abolished into the neighbouring western European countries, which created a huge wave of short attendees, particularly in Vienna in Austria and Germany.
Czechoslovakians could simply not get enough of their journeys to the West for a long time, everyone wanted to be there for at least a couple of days.
Czech holidaymakers slept when travelling to Paris in a tent in the park, because who would already have suffered their money for accommodation in the hotel or a guest house.
A typical characteristic feature of Tschechen's overseas travels included luggage room packed with preserves, beer and continuous sausage, etc.
Another important milestone was the introduction of freely exchangeable Czech crown - after decades our tourists were finally able to buy the western certificate.
Our standard of living increased as well as the average salary, which resulted in another important moment in 1997.
Thanks to increased inquiries regarding travel after 1997 the travel agenci agencies began preparing travel regularly through charter flights thanks to the possibility of negotiating quantity discounts incredibly cheaper compared to the past.
At the very end of the '90s, with the exception of the U.S. the Czechs had already non-fevisless payment transactions with all highly advanced countries around the world, which made travel even easier.
An other interesting moment in the history of "post-samten" travel occurred in the year 2000, when thanks to the severe great demand the first direct charter flight into an exotic country had been handled - Thailand.
Since the year every year direct charter aircraft with Czech passengers fly to distant sunny countries; this year twelve exotic destinations fly directly from the Czech Republic.
Each year's coast traveling to exotic countries at 100 thousands of riders!
An other important moment during the epoch characterized our "post-revolutionary" development came in the year 2008.
On 17 November 2008, the United States abolished the visa requirement for Czech tourists, which definitely included our rural advanced countries in the world.
In the age of 2008, the Czechs spent more than half their holidays abroad (50.3%) and, for the first occasions in their history, included more than €15 thousand for a single holiday including the price at the holiday resort.
