asphalt prevented by UNAM potholes
Rafael Herrera, researcher at the Faculty of chemischemistry at the Mexican university UNAM develops modified asphalt with polymers to make the compound more resistant to weather conditions and temperature fluctuations
Looking respected attention would be required to repair nearly 200,000 pot holes when the rainy season breaks at the Zona Metropolitana del Valle de México (city area of the valley of Mexico), Rafael Herrera Nájera said researchers at the Faculty of chemischemistry (FQ) at UNAM university, sworn and assured that when lifting and maintenance of asphalt surface is avoid.
During his lecture "Schlagholes from a technical point of view", which he delivered on the occasion of the International Year on Chemistry, he announced that the laboratory for which he is responsible are developing modified asphalt with polymers in order to produce a mixture similar to the asphalt, but which has greater mechanical resistance at both high and low temperatures.
Herrera Nájera considered this type of modified asphalt should be used in areas with an extremely hot climate, for example in some cities in the north of the country, in order to improve the behavior of road surface, thereby avoiding the occurrence of potholes.
He declared that the asphalt - the heaviest piece of crude oil is a hydrocarbon compound of which some has a high molecular weight - the so-called asphaltenes - and the other a low - the so-called Malthene; together they give the road surface its characteristic tough elastic behavior.
Toughlastic behavior of the asphalt means when high temperatures of nearly 180°C it behaves like a fluid, at 120°C it is very viscous and almost fixed at 50°C, while becoming brittle at very low temperatures.
"This means that the durability of the tarmac on the streets is not corresponding, but is changing one day depending on weather conditions and temperature fluctuations."
According to FQ explorers, the majority street streets of Mexico had a flexible road surface consisting of six types of raw materials that included the last three asphalt.
Between the upper shifts there is a wear layer made of asphalt, which consists of small piecemeals blazed in asphalt and then strengtmmed stone.
Each little stone of this wearing layer was bathed in asphalt.
When the vehicles move around and forces are generated, this layer enables a good distribution of strength in road surface.
"Whereas it included modified asphalt, the strengths would spread even better," said the chemical engineer.
"Above the wear layer," he elaborated, "a last layer is laid shaped, the so-called base layer made of tiny stones, i.e. sand, which was also bathed in asphalt.
Since this layer is in contact with the vehicle tyres, it requires greater mechanical strength than the wear slide layer and it needs friction.
While distributing this road surface most of the piecemeal forces required the base layer also require an appropriate thick thickness, and the material from which it consists must be maintained.
Weather conditions, especially humidity and temperature fluctuations, are further important factors to consider, especially the water," he said, "because significant impedes the behavior of the wear stratum.
When the terrain becomes damp, water penetrates the shift penetrates and occurs diffwhile, which causes a softening of all layers of road surface and, consequently, the occurrence of potholes.
When a beating hole occurs," he said, "the surface shift is initially removed while the wearing shift will only be affected after, but the damage occurs immediately occurring, it is a process that would have to be maintained by appropriate maintension programs at the initial indications."
Sistema Nacional de Investigadores (National Research System of Mexico) declared that the vehicle load would continue to cause hitting holes, which is why the thick thickness of each layer of road surface must always be related to vehicle load, ensuring that this scheme is maintained to avoid frequent pot holes.
Rafael Herrera indicated that a number of further investigations could still be achieved, for example through asphalt emulsions, which were already on the market in other countries, with which the potholes could easily be repaired without the application of high temperatures.
